```

That means:

* If the incoming request’s **path** is exactly `/json`, it will respond with JSON.
* For **anything else** (like `/`, `/favicon.ico`, etc.), it sends `404 Not Found`.

Now when you type `http://localhost:4000` in your browser:

* The browser sends a request to **`/`**, not `/json`.
* So your server goes to the `else` block:

```js
res.writeHead(404);
res.end("Not Found");
```

Hence: **"Not Found"**.

💡 **Fix it by adding a route for `/`:**

```js
if (req.url === "/json") {
  res.writeHead(200, { "Content-Type": "application/json" });
  res.end(JSON.stringify({ message: "Hello from server!" }));
} else if (req.url === "/") {
  res.writeHead(200, { "Content-Type": "text/plain" });
  res.end("Welcome to the homepage!");
} else {
  res.writeHead(404);
  res.end("Not Found");
}
```

