\#4. **Node.js File System & Streams — Performance, Memory, Backpressure**

This is where **real backend engineers are separated from script kiddies**.

---

## ✅ QUICK CORE

Streams in Node.js allow you to process **large files/data** piece-by-piece (**chunked**) without
 loading the entire thing into memory — perfect for **performance and scalability**.

---

## 🧠 DEEP HOOKS (with practical code)

---

### 1. **`fs.readFile()` vs Streams**

#### ❌ Using `fs.readFile()`:

```js
const fs = require('fs');
fs.readFile('bigfile.txt', 'utf8', (err, data) => {
  console.log(data); // Loads ENTIRE file into RAM!
});
```

📛 Bad for:

* Large files (e.g. 1GB logs)
* Concurrent access from many clients

---

#### ✅ Using Streams:

```js
const fs = require('fs');
const stream = fs.createReadStream('bigfile.txt', { encoding: 'utf8' });

stream.on('data', chunk => {
  console.log('Chunk:', chunk.length);
});

stream.on('end', () => {
  console.log('Done reading file.');
});
```

✔ This reads **small chunks** (default: 64KB)
✔ Ideal for real-time processing or piping

---

### 2. **Readable, Writable, Duplex, Transform**

| Stream Type | Example                |
| ----------- | ---------------------- |
| Readable    | `fs.createReadStream`  |
| Writable    | `fs.createWriteStream` |
| Duplex      | `net.Socket`           |
| Transform   | `zlib.createGzip()`    |

---

### 3. **Piping Streams (Built-in Backpressure)**

```js
const fs = require('fs');
const zlib = require('zlib');

const read = fs.createReadStream('big.txt');
const write = fs.createWriteStream('big.txt.gz');
const gzip = zlib.createGzip();

read.pipe(gzip).pipe(write);
```

✔ Each stream handles flow and pause internally
✔ If write speed is slower, read stream will **pause automatically** — this is called **backpressure**.

---

### 4. **Manual Backpressure Handling**

You can manually check return value of `write()`:

```js
const fs = require('fs');
const read = fs.createReadStream('file.txt');
const write = fs.createWriteStream('output.txt');

read.on('data', chunk => {
  const canWrite = write.write(chunk);
  if (!canWrite) {
    read.pause();
    write.once('drain', () => read.resume());
  }
});
```

📌 `write.write()` returns false when the internal buffer is full
📌 `drain` event signals when it's ready again

---

### 5. **Error Handling in Streams (MUST DO)**

```js
read.on('error', err => console.error('Read error:', err));
write.on('error', err => console.error('Write error:', err));
```

If you skip this → unhandled stream error → crash.

---

### 6. **`stream.pipeline()` — Safer Stream Composition**

```js
const { pipeline } = require('stream');
const fs = require('fs');
const zlib = require('zlib');

pipeline(
  fs.createReadStream('file.txt'),
  zlib.createGzip(),
  fs.createWriteStream('file.txt.gz'),
  err => {
    if (err) console.error('Pipeline failed:', err);
    else console.log('Pipeline succeeded.');
  }
);
```

✔ Handles error propagation automatically
✔ Recommended over raw `.pipe()` chains

---

## ❌ DEADLY TRAPS

| Trap                              | Result                |
| --------------------------------- | --------------------- |
| Using `readFile()` on large files | Out-of-memory crash   |
| Not handling stream errors        | Crashes process       |
| Not managing backpressure         | Slow, unstable app    |
| Using `.pipe()` without `error`   | Silent data loss risk |

---

## 🧠 Real Interview Q: Streaming CSV Parsing

```js
const csv = require('csv-parser');
const fs = require('fs');

fs.createReadStream('data.csv')
  .pipe(csv())
  .on('data', (row) => console.log(row))
  .on('end', () => console.log('CSV processed'));
```

✔ Efficiently processes line-by-line
✔ Use this in real APIs (e.g. file uploads, logs)

---

## 🗣 BATTLE PHRASE:

> “I always use streams for memory-sensitive operations and prefer `pipeline()` for fault-tolerant composition with built-in backpressure handling.”

